WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.870
-My first guest is a Tony,
Oscar, and Grammy-winning

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:09.475
music legend who has sold over
300 million records worldwide.

00:00:09.475 --> 00:00:12.045
Holy moly. 300 million.

00:00:12.045 --> 00:00:14.046
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.046 --> 00:00:17.517
His new album, "The Lockdown
Sessions," is out now.

00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:20.987
Please welcome the one and only
Elton John!

00:00:20.987 --> 00:00:22.489
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:22.489 --> 00:00:24.724
Whoa!
That's what I'm talking about!

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:26.493
Yes!

00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:29.395
Ah, it's so good to see you.

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:31.430
-Thank you.
Lovely to see you, Jimmy.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:32.765
-Thank you so much
for being here.

00:00:32.765 --> 00:00:34.033
I know you're in London.

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:36.368
So I appreciate you Zooming in
and talking to me.

00:00:36.368 --> 00:00:37.937
I just want to tell you
how much I love

00:00:37.937 --> 00:00:39.972
the album,
"The Lockdown Sessions."

00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:43.242
And congrats on having
a number 1 with Dua Lipa.

00:00:43.242 --> 00:00:47.046
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:47.046 --> 00:00:50.883
You're always doing something
different and I just love --

00:00:50.883 --> 00:00:53.920
You know I'm a big fan,
but I love when you get together

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.389
with Dua and that came out,
I go, "'Cold Heart'?

00:00:56.389 --> 00:00:58.824
What is going on? This is, like,
the coolest thing ever."

00:00:58.824 --> 00:01:00.126
And you didn't even
get to the --

00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:02.595
♪ It's no sacrifice ♪

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:04.097
You didn't even do that.
-Nope.

00:01:04.097 --> 00:01:06.432
-No.
-No, we cut that bit out.

00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:07.834
-Yeah, you didn't need it.

00:01:07.834 --> 00:01:09.035
You just had "cold, cold heart."

00:01:09.035 --> 00:01:11.303
I go, "Ah."
I just loved it so much.

00:01:11.303 --> 00:01:13.939
Congrats.
-Thank you very much. Thank you.

00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:16.943
-Did you know you were going to
put an album out?

00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:19.345
-Listen, it all came about
by accident.

00:01:19.345 --> 00:01:23.282
During lockdown, I was
in this house in Windsor

00:01:23.282 --> 00:01:25.919
in England with my family,
with the kids and David.

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:27.220
And the last thing I thought

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.155
I was going to do
is make any music.

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:31.290
And then people asked me
to start playing

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:32.959
and singing on
their own records,

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:35.028
which I -- you know,
that's how I started out.

00:01:35.028 --> 00:01:38.030
I was a session guy
before I became Elton.

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:41.567
So I played on a Rina Sawayama,
a Gorillaz thing, a Metallica,

00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:46.839
Miley Cyrus track,
Glen Campbell, Lil Nas X,

00:01:46.839 --> 00:01:49.141
Years &amp; Years,
and I thought, "I like this."

00:01:49.141 --> 00:01:51.311
I like this, going back to
where I used to be.

00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:54.280
-Yeah. Then you're like, "Why
don't you come on my album?"

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.614
-Yeah. [ Laughs ]

00:01:55.614 --> 00:01:57.917
Well, when I did the Lil Nas X,
Glen Campbell,

00:01:57.917 --> 00:02:00.486
I was in Studio 2 at Abbey Road.

00:02:00.486 --> 00:02:03.890
And 54 years, practically to
the day, I was playing piano

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:06.059
on The Hollies' "He Ain't Heavy,
He's My Brother."

00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:09.628
So that's kind of a nice feeling
to have.

00:02:09.628 --> 00:02:11.197
-That is pretty cool.

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:14.133
I was surprised to find out
but I guess it's true that --

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:15.434
'cause Stevie Wonder is on here.

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:18.171
Everybody is on this album,
by the way.

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:20.105
This is an awesome gift,
by the way.

00:02:20.105 --> 00:02:22.242
Because if you're like,
"Oh, yeah, I love --"

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:25.211
Obviously, I love Elton John,
but if you love Stevie Wonder --

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:27.580
You guys have never done a duet
together.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:28.981
-No, we've never done a duet.

00:02:28.981 --> 00:02:31.250
And we've known each other
for so long.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.319
And so, when I wrote this song,

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:36.522
I suddenly thought there's
a harmonica solo here.

00:02:36.522 --> 00:02:38.691
And then he came
and played piano.

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:42.328
And then we put drums and bass
and then the choir.

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:43.896
And in the end, he sang on it.

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:45.398
And it's a dream come true
for me

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:48.501
because, you know, not only do
I love him as a musician

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:50.536
and worship him as a songwriter,

00:02:50.536 --> 00:02:52.839
but he's one of my favorite
people in the whole wide world.

00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:56.375
So this was a very exciting
thing for me.

00:02:56.375 --> 00:02:59.312
-I have a photo here from 1973.

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:02.114
And I think there's a cool story
behind it.

00:03:02.114 --> 00:03:05.317
Do you remember where this was
or what this was?

00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:06.719
-I do remember.

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:10.122
It's not one of my brightest
and most wonderful moments.

00:03:10.122 --> 00:03:13.960
I was on "The Starship," which
was a jet that we were renting.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.395
I had just done a show.
It was birthday.

00:03:16.395 --> 00:03:18.064
And I wasn't happy with
the show.

00:03:18.064 --> 00:03:20.666
So I went up front
and sat on my own.

00:03:20.666 --> 00:03:22.768
And they said,
"Come up to the bow.

00:03:22.768 --> 00:03:24.003
We want to sing 'Happy Birthday'
to you."

00:03:24.003 --> 00:03:26.004
And I went, "I'm not interested
in my birthday."

00:03:26.004 --> 00:03:27.907
And this happened about
three or four times until

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:30.610
my publicist burst into tears
and said,

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:33.712
"You have to come up there.
Stevie Wonder is at the organ."

00:03:33.712 --> 00:03:35.214
Of course, the plane
had an organ on it.

00:03:35.214 --> 00:03:36.415
[ Laughter ]
"And he wants to sing

00:03:36.415 --> 00:03:39.451
'Happy Birthday' to you."
And I felt such a horror.

00:03:39.451 --> 00:03:41.954
I felt so bad
and I went up there,

00:03:41.954 --> 00:03:43.756
and he sang "Happy Birthday"
to me.

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:47.026
And it's an incident
I will never forget,

00:03:47.026 --> 00:03:48.794
and as I say,
I'm not very proud of.

00:03:48.794 --> 00:03:51.430
-No, yeah, but it's still cool
that, first of all,

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.800
the name of the private plane
was called "The Starship."

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:56.335
I love that, and then the fact
that you have a --

00:03:56.335 --> 00:03:59.004
of course you have
a piano on the plane

00:03:59.004 --> 00:04:00.540
or an organ on the plane.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.007
-Yeah.
-That's pretty rad.

00:04:02.007 --> 00:04:04.777
Have you ever surprised anyone
with a happy birthday?

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:07.146
By singing them
a "Happy Birthday" song?

00:04:07.146 --> 00:04:09.115
-I can't remember.
I must have in my life.

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:12.318
But Stevie actually sang
"Happy Birthday" to me

00:04:12.318 --> 00:04:14.319
at my 70th birthday as well,

00:04:14.319 --> 00:04:18.791
along with Lady Gaga,
Ryan Adams, and Rosanne Cash.

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:20.826
So, I mean, I've been very lucky
in that respect.

00:04:20.826 --> 00:04:22.795
And at my 60th birthday,
Tony Bennett,

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:24.897
the Scissor Sisters,
and Mary J. Blige.

00:04:24.897 --> 00:04:26.832
So I've been quite lucky
with people singing to me,

00:04:26.832 --> 00:04:28.701
but I'm not very good
at singing birthdays.

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:31.003
I haven't really sung at
anybody else's birthday party,

00:04:31.003 --> 00:04:32.704
but I've had lots of people
singing to me.

00:04:32.704 --> 00:04:34.674
So I'm very lucky.
-Yeah.

00:04:34.674 --> 00:04:36.342
Mine's coming up September.

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:37.442
[ Laughter ]
So I will just...

00:04:37.442 --> 00:04:38.644
-Okay.
-...throw it out there,

00:04:38.644 --> 00:04:40.279
and we'll see where you're at.

00:04:40.279 --> 00:04:42.749
-Well, just give me an e-mail
and I'll see where I am.

00:04:42.749 --> 00:04:43.883
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.883 --> 00:04:45.851
-Yeah, no problem.

00:04:45.851 --> 00:04:48.087
You know, last time we talked,

00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:50.956
I was talking about how you
always are into new music.

00:04:50.956 --> 00:04:52.959
And you're always supporting
new artists,

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:56.495
which you do as well on this,
not only Dua Lipa,

00:04:56.495 --> 00:04:58.998
but also you have Young Thug
on here.

00:04:58.998 --> 00:05:01.267
You always know about music
before it comes out,

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:02.568
which is opposite of me.

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:03.736
I'm going backwards.

00:05:03.736 --> 00:05:05.138
I'm just finding old music
and going like,

00:05:05.138 --> 00:05:06.505
"Have you guys heard this song?"

00:05:06.505 --> 00:05:08.941
And everyone's like, "Yeah,
it came out 30 years ago."

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:12.144
But how do you stay up to date

00:05:12.144 --> 00:05:15.381
and why has it become
a cool new tradition for you?

00:05:15.381 --> 00:05:16.782
You've been calling
all these people

00:05:16.782 --> 00:05:19.017
and just saying hi
and that you're a fan.

00:05:19.017 --> 00:05:20.986
-Well, I do a radio show
on Apple

00:05:20.986 --> 00:05:22.555
every week for six years now.

00:05:22.555 --> 00:05:25.057
Every week for six years,
called "The Rocket Hour."

00:05:25.057 --> 00:05:26.792
And during that hour,

00:05:26.792 --> 00:05:30.129
I try and play new music that
hasn't been played before.

00:05:30.129 --> 00:05:33.365
And so, you know,
I get to hear new music,

00:05:33.365 --> 00:05:34.600
and then when I love something,

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.902
I have the person on
to interview them

00:05:36.902 --> 00:05:38.303
and I get to know them.

00:05:38.303 --> 00:05:41.107
On the album, there's Olly
Alexander from Years &amp; Years,

00:05:41.107 --> 00:05:45.277
Rina Sawayama,
Surfaces, and SG Lewis.

00:05:45.277 --> 00:05:47.246
And I've gotten to be friends
with all these artists.

00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:49.181
You know,
ever since I was a kid,

00:05:49.181 --> 00:05:51.517
I've been a fan,
and I love records.

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:54.253
And I know the new.
I know the old.

00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:55.921
But I'm more interested in
the new

00:05:55.921 --> 00:05:58.791
because that's what's going to
take music further.

00:05:58.791 --> 00:06:01.193
And a lot of these people
don't get enough exposure

00:06:01.193 --> 00:06:03.929
because radios have
so many definite playlists

00:06:03.929 --> 00:06:05.931
and these songs don't fit in.

00:06:05.931 --> 00:06:08.134
And so if I can get a chance
to promote them

00:06:08.134 --> 00:06:10.769
and then be friends with them
and then play with them

00:06:10.769 --> 00:06:13.573
and record with them,
then it's fantastic for me.

00:06:13.573 --> 00:06:14.773
-Do you stay friends with --

00:06:14.773 --> 00:06:17.576
[ Applause ]
Absolutely.

00:06:17.576 --> 00:06:20.546
That's why you're the -- Do you
stay in touch with these people?

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:22.348
Do you actually stay friends
with them?

00:06:22.348 --> 00:06:24.984
-Yeah, yesterday,
I rang Teyana Taylor,

00:06:24.984 --> 00:06:29.088
who was a guest on my show
a year and a half ago.

00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:31.991
I did a video with her
because we became friends.

00:06:31.991 --> 00:06:35.060
Yes, I do, and I think it's
really good and it's important.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:37.863
Jimmy, when I came to America,
I had so much kindness

00:06:37.863 --> 00:06:41.333
shown towards me by
Brian Wilson, the Beach Boys,

00:06:41.333 --> 00:06:44.203
Neil Diamond, Leon Russell,
the Beatles,

00:06:44.203 --> 00:06:48.274
Bob Dylan, Linda Ronstadt,
The Band,

00:06:48.274 --> 00:06:50.042
and that made me feel so good.

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:52.545
It kind of ratified
what I was doing.

00:06:52.545 --> 00:06:56.849
And so I always thought I must
carry on that tradition

00:06:56.849 --> 00:06:59.718
of being, if you like something,
tell the person that it's good.

00:06:59.718 --> 00:07:01.020
It doesn't have to be
a musician.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.722
Could be a photographer,
a painter, an actor, whatever.

00:07:03.722 --> 00:07:06.859
But if it gives you pleasure
and it makes you feel good,

00:07:06.859 --> 00:07:08.193
then ring them up and tell them.

00:07:08.193 --> 00:07:10.329
-Yeah, I love that.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:10.329 --> 00:07:12.064
More with Elton John
after the break.

00:07:12.064 --> 00:07:14.033
We're going to talk about
his tour. Come on back.

